kosha drawing-varga

  -- engineering drawing: the precise visual language of engineering.
  -- not artistic drawing — communicative drawing. every line means something.
  -- a drawing is a specification: it tells a manufacturer exactly what to make.
  -- ambiguity in a drawing = defective part = structural failure = liability.
  --
  -- two types:
  --   orthographic projection: the object shown from multiple views simultaneously.
  --     front view, top view, side view — three views fully define any 3D object.
  --     first-angle projection (Europe, Asia): view placed opposite to direction of sight.
  --     third-angle projection (USA, Canada): view placed same side as direction of sight.
  --
  --   isometric / perspective: 3D representation on 2D paper.
  --     isometric: all axes at 120°, no perspective distortion. measurable.
  --     used for assembly drawings, installation guides, exploded views.
  --
  -- what a drawing must convey:
  --   geometry:     shape, size, proportions.
  --   dimensions:   exact measurements with tolerances.
  --   tolerances:   how much variation is acceptable. ±0.1mm, ±0.001mm etc.
  --   surface finish: roughness Ra value. important for mating parts.
  --   material:     what it is made of. heat treatment if any.
  --   title block:  name, date, scale, revision, drawn by, approved by.
  --   bill of materials: list of all parts, quantities, materials.
  --
  -- standards:
  --   ISO 128: technical drawing conventions (international).
  --   ASME Y14.5: geometric dimensioning and tolerancing (GD&T) — USA.
  --   BS 8888: UK standard.
  --   GD&T: a symbolic language for tolerances — flatness, roundness,
  --          position, perpendicularity, parallelism, concentricity.
  --
  -- the drawing as proof:
  --   a signed and sealed engineering drawing is a legal document.
  --   it certifies that the design meets specifications.
  --   the professional engineer's stamp is the siddhi (the proof mark).

  "engineering-varga-vishesa"
  "subanta-swarupa"

  "geometry-yukta"             -- shape is the foundation
  "dimension-yukta"            -- measurement makes it precise
  "tolerance-yukta"            -- variation bounds make it manufacturable
  "projection-yukta"           -- orthographic and isometric
  "standard-yukta"             -- ISO, ASME, BS standards

  -- math connections
  "math-varga-yukta"
  "coordinate-yukta"           -- drawing lives in coordinate space
  "vector-yukta"               -- direction and magnitude of lines

  -- 3d / pratibimba connection
  "domain-3d-yukta"            -- CAD is engineering drawing in 3D

